OpenRevo EC driver for Ubuntu and Debian
======================================

This package contains the source for the Uniwill WMI OpenRevo EC driver.
It requires headers matching the kernel you intend to use. DKMS builds and
installs the module for available kernels and rebuilds it after kernel upgrades.
Read installation output: missing kernel headers can defer the module build.

Secure Boot signing uses the distribution's DKMS configuration and keys.
Complete its MOK enrollment procedure if requested. This package does not
disable Secure Boot, generate its own signing policy, or enroll keys silently.

The application and driver must support the same transport and cooling ABIs. Close the
application and reboot after an upgrade to activate the newly built module.
Package installation and removal never force an active module to unload.

Old manual installations under /lib/modules/<kernel>/extra/openrevo must be
resolved before configuring this package. Its maintainer scripts report such
files and leave them intact. Do not operate two copies of the controller.

Run the graphical application as the desktop user. The udev rule grants device
access to the active local session. The driver binds MECHREVO vendor systems exposing the Uniwill EC WMI GUID;
there is no board/BIOS allowlist. A matching GUID is not validation of every
machine's register contract. The app validates factory tables before writes.
The force=1 module option bypasses only the vendor check, not the WMI GUID or
register whitelist. It is not enabled by this package.

Raw writes are restricted to the shared registry whitelist. Mode and CPU
transactions and rollback run in Rust. Kernel fan ownership restores curves
and boost after release, lease expiry, suspend, or removal; failed restores
are retried while the device is available. Process crashes do not replay an
unfinished Rust CPU/mode transaction.

/dev/openrevo_events is a separate read-only, single-reader raw WMI stream.
It exposes event sequence numbers and codes, without dispatching key actions.
If a system driver (such as uniwill-wmi) already owns the event GUID, this node
may be absent even when EC controls work. No existing driver is unbound.
The udev rules grant the active local session access to both device nodes.

Device controls require device_controls_abi=1. Battery presets and custom
thresholds require the EC capability flag and valid existing policy. No platform
identity spoofing is performed. Device writes are blocked while a custom fan
curve is active. Win-lock pulses are never automatically repeated on timeout.
Physical mode key linkage is opt-in in the app settings (or CLI hotkeys on).
